# Does Radio Advertising Still Work? Why Audio Belongs in Your Media Mix

"Nobody listens to the radio anymore." We hear some version of this all the time — usually from the same people who've never actually tried it. The truth is more interesting, and a lot more useful: audio advertising never went away. It evolved, and for the right business, it's one of the highest-leverage channels you can buy.

At One Take Wonder Marketing, we've been placing audio for local and regional businesses for more than a decade. We've watched streaming audio grow into a precision-targeting machine while terrestrial radio quietly kept its hold on the morning commute. Here's what actually works in audio advertising today — and why it still deserves a serious look.

## The Reach That Still Surprises People

Radio still reaches the vast majority of American adults every single week. That's a bigger weekly audience than most social platforms, and it happens in a place where your message faces almost no competition for attention: the car.

Think about your own day. During the morning commute, your customer isn't scrolling past your ad at 40 miles an hour. They're listening. Radio and streaming audio own the drive time, the gym session, and the workday playlist — the moments when eyes are busy but ears are free. That's captive attention you simply can't buy on a screen.

And audio isn't standing still. Streaming audio and podcasts have exploded, and with them came something terrestrial radio never had: the ability to target listeners by location, age, interests, and even the music they already love. You can now run an audio ad that reaches a specific neighborhood, at a specific time of day, with a message built for that exact listener.

## Why Audio Works When Other Channels Get Tuned Out

People skip video ads. They scroll past banner ads without seeing them. They've trained their eyes to ignore the noise. But they don't skip the radio — they turn it up.

Audio has three advantages screens will never match. First, it's intimate. A voice in your ear lands differently than a headline on a page; it feels like a recommendation, not an interruption. Second, it's memorable. A well-produced spot with the right music and a clear message sticks in a way a static banner never will. Third, it's consistent. Audio rewards frequency — the same message, repeated across weeks, builds the kind of top-of-mind awareness that makes a customer reach for your name when they finally need you.

None of that requires a blockbuster budget. Local radio and streaming audio are often far more affordable than most business owners assume, especially for a focused geographic area.

## The Audio Landscape in One Breath

"Audio advertising" isn't one thing anymore. It's four, and they each earn their keep differently:

- **Terrestrial radio** — unmatched for broad local reach and credibility. Being "on the radio" still carries weight, and it's the backbone of building a brand in a specific market.
- **Streaming audio** — think Spotify, Pandora, and iHeartRadio. Precision targeting and measurable performance, delivered to a listener who's actively choosing what they hear.
- **Podcasts** — hosts who've earned deep trust with a loyal audience. A host-read ad can feel like a personal recommendation from a friend.
- **Satellite radio** — national reach for brands that need to go beyond a single region.

The mistake most businesses make is picking one and calling it done. The real power comes from weaving them together — radio for reach and recall, streaming for targeting, podcasts for trust — all reinforcing one consistent message.

## What Makes Audio Advertising Actually Work

Plenty of businesses have "tried radio once" and decided it didn't work. Almost every time, the problem wasn't the medium — it was the execution. Here's what separates audio that pays for itself from audio that just makes noise:

**Frequency over one-offs.** A single spot is a lottery ticket. Audio works through repetition. The listeners who become customers are the ones who've heard you enough times that your name is the first one they think of.

**One clear message.** You have thirty seconds. Don't try to say three things. Say one thing — a single, specific offer or promise — and repeat it. If they remember one phrase, the ad did its job.

**A memorable sonic identity.** A distinctive voice, a jingle, or a signature sound — these are what make your brand recognizable the moment the ad starts, before a single word is spoken.

**A reason to act now.** "Visit our website," "Call today," "Mention this ad" — give the listener a next step, and make it easy. A vanity URL or a dedicated phone number gives them somewhere to go.

## Measuring What You Can't See

The oldest knock on radio is that it's hard to measure. It used to be true. It isn't anymore.

A dedicated call-tracking number tells you exactly how many calls a spot generated. A vanity URL tied to your campaign shows you the web traffic that followed. A promo code turns a listener into a data point. And here's the one most businesses miss: watch what happens to your branded search after a campaign launches. When audio does its job, people don't always click a link — they Google your name. A spike in branded search is audio working, in real time.

That's the measurement that matters: not just how many people heard you, but how many reached for you.

## Where Audio Fits in Your Media Mix

Audio isn't an either/or. It's a force multiplier.

The most effective campaigns we run at One Take Wonder Marketing don't rely on any single channel — they build on each other. A radio spot builds awareness and recall. That awareness makes your search ads cheaper and more effective, because people search for names they've already heard. Your social ads then retarget the people who visited. Your website closes the deal. Audio is often the first touch that makes every later touch work harder.

That's the thing about a full-service agency: you're not buying a radio campaign. You're buying a media mix with a strategy behind it — and audio is one of the sharpest tools in that kit.

